<BaseLayout title="Intro 101" backHref={`${base}guides/`} backLabel="Guides">
  <h1>World of Warships: Legends - Intro 101</h1>
  <h5><i>This page is 100% Human Authored</i></h5>

  <p>Ready to get quickly up to speed on the basics of this game?  Start here:</p>
  
  <h2>It's a Tactical Shooter</h2>
  <p>
    This is something that's important to keep in the back of your mind as you start to learn the game.  
    While "tactics" may be beyond the scope of an intro guide, I think it's important to know right off
    the bat that games are won by long term (in game terms) choices, not twitchy fingers or perfect accuracy with the guns / torpedoes.
    Think of it like American Football: Yes the fundamentals (throwing, catching) are table stakes for playing well, but the actual game
    is the choices the team makes.
  </p>
  <p>
    I'll leave it at this for this guide:  Running around guns-blazing engaging in any ship within range like it's a typical 
    multiplayer shooting game = you're sunk.
  </p>

  <h2>Ship Classes</h2>
  <p>
    There's variance of course within a given class but the following is generally true:
  </p>
  <h3>Destroyers</h3>
  <p>
    Small, fast, low HP, short range guns, hard to see, hard to hit.  
    Uses agility and smoke screens and their high top speed to avoid being quickly wiped out.  
    Their role:
    <ol>
      <li>Spot ships (more on what this means later)</li>
      <li>Capture areas</li>
      <li>Support slower teammates (typicallly smoke)</li>
      <li>Fight battleships</li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <h3>Cruisers</h3>
  <p>
    Medium in almost every dimension.  Eg: More HP than a Destroyer, less than a Battlship.  
    Higher calliber guns than a Destroyer but less than a Battlsship.  Faster reload than a Battleship but slower than a Destroyer.
    On so on.   They are the backbone of any fleet.  They often have the best selection of consumables and ships within this class
    can vary a lot, from glass cannon snipers to tough close range brawlers to support ships.
  </p>

  <h3>Battleships</h3>
  <p>
    Big, slow, high HP, long range guns, easy to see, easy to hit.  
    The opposite of a Destroyer.  Often lack torpedoes entirely.
    Their role:
    <ol>
      <li>
        Tank.  High visibility, high HP, great armor.  They can absorb many times the number of hits of Cruisers and Destroyers before sinking,
        plus many have consumables that repairs light damage, further increasing their total HP pool.  Many have good belt armor 
        (the armor at the waterline that torpedoes will hit).
      </li>
      <li>
        Fight cruisers.  While Destroyers are very difficult to hit with a Battleship,
        a well placed shot or two can shred a lot of cruisers.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  
  <p>
    As a general guideline, each ship class is weak to a class and strong against a class:
    <ul>
      <li>
        Battleships generally outgun Cruisers, particularly at close range.  The high calliber of the typical Battleship's 
        main guns is enough to really mess up a lot of Cruisers.
      </li>
      <li>
        Cruisers generally outgun Destroyers.  Agility has less to do with it, it's a simple matter of relative HP pool sizes, 
        relative armor thickness, relative gun calliber, etc.
      </li>
      <li>
        Destroyers generally outgun Battleships.  This is the unusal paring, as one might assume that 'bigger = better' 
        and since Battlships are the biggest class of regular ship and Destroyers are the smallest, that Battleships beat Destroyers.
        The reason it doesn't work that way is that Battleships are <i>so much slower</i> and due to their slow-reloading speed 
        and slow turret rotation speed, Destroyers simply dance around them, with the Battleship captain struggling to land a single
        shot on the Destroyer, while the Destroyer has a nice big easy target for it's torpedoes (main gun fire from a Destroyer
        is not typically effective against Battleships).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

</BaseLayout>
